      '**No table.** A Q table has a value for every situation and move. There are 20,358,520 six-card hands; with whose crib it is, 40.7 million situations to throw from, and 15 throws each (cell 1). 8,000 training hands would visit at most one in 5,090 of those situations, about once each: nothing could be learned. Lesson 9.8 showed the answer: describe each move by a few features, and learn one weight per feature, so what is learned in one hand applies to every hand with similar numbers.',
      "**What makes a good feature.** It is a number about the move in its situation that makes the move better or worse more or less in proportion (more points now: better; a bigger chance the other player scores next: worse). It means the same thing in every hand. It uses only what this player has seen: its own cards, the starter, the cards played, never the other hand. And it is scaled to about 0 to 1, so no one feature's size swamps the others when the weights are learned (cell 5: most run 0 to 1, a few up to 2 or 3).",
      "**The throw's features.** For each of the 15 throws: 1 (a bias), the expected show of the four kept / 10 (lesson 10.8's average over the 46 starters), the crib value of the two thrown / 10, + if the crib is mine and − if it is theirs, and ±1 for whose crib it is. From 5 5 J K 2 9, throwing 2 9 keeps the most (1.24, 12.4 points) and throwing 5 5 gives the crib the most (0.90) (cell 2). The weights will decide how to trade one against the other.",
