      "**Why features that do not differ between moves?** The last three cannot help choose a card: every card has the same values. They are there so that Q can predict how many points are still to come in the hand, which the show decides. That matters for learning: the throw learns its value from the value of the first pegging move (lesson 10.11's target). If no pegging feature said how good the crib was, the crib value the throw created would be lost one step later, and the AI would learn to undervalue cribs. In the training runs that built this game, the throw's crib weight stayed near 2 (against 3.6 to 6.8 for the kept hand, where both should be about equal) until the crib feature was added to the pegging block and the step size started larger (lesson 10.11); then it learned 5.7 against 10.2.",
